#import "../template.typ": *

= Dynamic movement

A dynamic move uses #jt("momentum") (the speed your body carries) to reach a hold. Your legs and hips throw your body, so your hand arrives while you are still moving. This chapter runs from the small #ix("Deadpoint")deadpoint on a V3 to the jumps of #jt("comp style", body: [comp-style]) setting. #ix("Comp-style setting")Comp style means problems set like competition boulders, with runs, jumps and big shapes.

If you climb V4 but not yet V5, start here. Gyms set more competition moves for ordinary climbers than they used to. #jt("setter", body: [Setters]) (the staff who design the problems) call a move "compy" when it is dynamic and coordinated: "usually runs, jumps or dynos" #src("D32"). World Cup holds now turn up on gym V2s #src("D33").

If you're tall, you reach many holds that shorter climbers must jump for#ix("Tall climbers!dynamic moves") #src("D45"), and the missing skill shows when a setter forces momentum (@cons-tall). Whatever your height, read @cons-falling before your first dyno or running jump.

The map shows this chapter's entries by grade. A line joins techniques that build on each other, and #link(<technique-map>)[the technique map] has all five chapters on one page.

#technique-map(only: "dynamic")

== Static or dynamic? <dynamic-choice>

You can stop a #ix("Static movement")static move, such as a #tref("lock-off", body: [lock-off]), halfway and go back. Once a #ix("Dynamic movement")dynamic move starts, you catch the hold or come off #src("D18"). In return, one coach's training-plan page calls momentum a big energy-saver #src("D38"), and moving faster can mean less time on the wall #src("D13"). Lattice Training's#ix("Lattice Training") advice is to match your speed to the problem: some want control, others fast moves #src("D41").

Choose a static move, or a very small deadpoint, when:

- the target is small or awkward, such as a thin edge, a finger hole or a slot. Dynos to small pockets are a common cause of finger injuries#ix("Injuries!finger") #src("D21").
- a fall would be dangerous, for example onto an awkward landing#ix("Falling!judging the landing") or into a #tref("volume", body: [volume], show-page: false) (a big hollow shape bolted to the wall) #src("D20").
- your body must end in an exact position, as on a #tref("slab", body: [slab], show-page: false) (a wall that leans back) #src("D13").

Otherwise, try the move dynamically first, with the smallest deadpoint that reaches. A physiotherapist who treats climbers says that dynamic climbing, done well, can be more efficient than static climbing without adding injury risk. The deadpoint is that article's example #src("D60"). One gym owner's article agrees that dynamic moves are generally more efficient than lock-offs #src("D13"). Build up to big jumps. The same physiotherapist says to start with small dynamic moves and move on to bigger ones over time #src("D60"). Another warns that too much dynamic climbing can shock-load the body #src("G66"). Don't snatch at small holds, because grabbing a hold very quickly in a dynamic move can rupture a #jt("pulley") (a band that holds a finger tendon against the bone) #src("C25"). Strictly, a move like this is a #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint]) while a foot stays on and a #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) once both feet leave #src("D2", "D9"). In the gym the names blur: many climbers call a #tref("lunge", body: [one-handed dyno]) with a foot still on a deadpoint #src("D15").

#ix("Deadpoint!origin of the term")The name describes the moment. Throw a ball straight up, and at the top it stops for an instant before it falls. Your hips do the same when you throw them up the wall: near the top you are barely moving and feel light, so the hold takes your weight gently.#note[The sources name this moment. The account of why the catch is gentle there is our own.] Grabbing at that instant is the deadpoint #src("D1", "D13"), a word John Gill#ix("Gill, John") used in print in 1969 #src("D4").

== Power comes from the legs and hips

Coaches agree: think of your "arms as a pivot and your legs as a spring" #src("D21", "D30").

+ *Start with straight arms.* A bent arm at launch has already spent its power #src("D13", "D16"). See #tref("straight-arms").
+ *Load.* Let your hips sag out from the wall and down. Move them away from where you will go #src("D10"). See #tref("pre-loading").
+ *Drive, and pull last.* Push with your legs and swing your hips back into the wall towards the target. Your body turns upward around your hands #src("D10", "D16"). Your arms join in once your hips are moving #src("D10").
+ *Keep the hips close.* Turn the hip on the throwing side to the wall. That raises the same shoulder and keeps you close on steep walls #src("D6", "D7"). See #tref("hip-turn"). If your hips drift off the wall, you fall short however hard you push #src("D10", "D17").

== Momentum and timing

#ix("Momentum")
- *Pump once at most, then don't pause.* Once you start, any stop kills your speed #src("D13", "D15"). See #tref("hip-pump").
- *Let go late.* Keep pushing on the start holds until the movement pulls them from your hands #src("D17", "D21").
- *Use only the speed you need.* Extra speed gives you a bigger swing to hold #src("D20").
- *Keep your eyes on the target* through the whole move #src("D21").
- *Think in three phases:* set up, launch, catch #src("D30"). Most failures happen in the first or the last. The catch is #tref("latching").

#readbox(title: [Is this move meant to be dynamic?], icon: none)[
  #ix("Route reading!dynamic moves")
  *Look for*
  - a gap with no holds in between. The next hold is out of reach on straight arms from your best stance. No foothold lets you walk your feet up first.
  - a lack of footholds where you need them: a blank panel, a single low foothold, or a hold or volume with grip on only some faces (#jt("dual-tex"), two textures) #src("D35").
  - a target made to be caught, such as a big, deep hold or two holds side by side. Small, far-off edges and pockets usually mean a deadpoint or a static move #src("D21").
  - a hold you can only hold if you arrive moving into it, such as a rounded hold or one you squeeze.
  - spread-out starts: start holds set wide, a #jt("four-point start") (a marked hold for each hand and foot), or a high start hold with clear floor in front #src("D35").
  - a row of holds or volumes at the same height #src("D31").

  *Symptoms*
  - You reach the hold slowly but can't hold the lock-off long enough to grip it, or your fingers slide off it. Try a #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint]).
  - The move dies halfway because you paused.
  - Your arms tire fast between decent holds, because you pulled with them instead of pushing with your legs #src("D6").
  - You stick the hold and the swing rips you off. You needed #tref("cutting-loose"), #tref("foot-catch") or a second hand.
]

== V3: momentum in everyday moves

#technique(
  key: "deadpoint",
  name: "Deadpoint",
  aliases: ("dead point", "slap", "dynamic layback", "toter Punkt", "hip pop"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 3,
  grade-note: [Try small ones from V2 on big holds. France's climbing federation teaches controlled dynamic moves at about that grade #src("F60", "F61"). A German gym chain teaches dynamic moves to climbers stepping up to Font 6A, which is V3 #src("F68", "F69"). Other gyms teach the deadpoint in intermediate classes, after about two months of climbing #src("D39"). One still teaches it to climbers stuck at V5 #src("F35").],
  summary: [You throw your hips up the wall and grab the next hold at the #cue[top of that movement], when you are barely moving and #cue[feel light]. At least one foot stays on #src("D1", "D9", "D13").],
  figure: "deadpoint.svg",
  caption: [(a) Load: keep your arms straight and let your hips sag out. (b) Drive your hips in and up. Grab at the top of the arc.],
  how: [
    + Stand on the big-toe edge of the push foot, below the target and a little to one side. #tref("flag", body: [Flag]) the other leg or put it on a foothold on the other side #src("D13").
    + Straighten both arms and let your hips sag out from the wall and slightly down #src("D10").
    + In one movement, drive up through the standing leg as if jumping and arch your back to bring your hips to the wall. A pause anywhere kills the move #src("D13"). Keep the holding arm straight while your hips rise, and bend it only to let your body pass.
    + As the rise slows, turn the reaching shoulder to the wall and send your hand 2–3 cm above the hold, not at its edge #src("D16"). Close your hand at the top, then push down through your foot so it stays on #src("D21").
  ],
  parts: (
    (
      name: [Hip pop],
      how: [
        A hip pop is a deadpoint thrown from a #tref("backstep", body: [backstep]) or a #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knee]). The hip turned to the wall rotates in and up, which throws the hand on that side much farther than square hips can #src("B41").
        + Set a backstep, with the outside edge of the shoe on the hold, or a partial drop knee, so that the hip on that side turns to the wall #src("D10").
        + Keep the holding arm straight and let your hips sag away from the wall #src("D10").
        + Push off the foot and rotate the hip into the wall and up in one snap #src("D10"). Turn towards the reaching arm as you go: right hip in, right shoulder up #src("D7"). Throw the hand at the top of the rise, with the arm at full length #src("D7", "D13").
        + On steep walls, avoid a foot that sits "behind #jt("the line")". Turn it into a drop knee instead #src("D19").

        *Don't throw from a deep drop knee.* The authors of a knee-injury study suspect that the injury comes at the moment a hand lets go, which adds load to the legs #src("G9"). The coach Dave MacLeod#ix("MacLeod, Dave") warns against dropping the knee and moving the hand in one rapid motion #src("D61"). See the knee box in @cons-injuries.
      ],
      figure: "hip-pop.svg",
      caption: [(a) Set a drop knee and let your hips sag. (b) Snap the right hip into the wall and up. The right hand throws.],
    ),
  ),
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - a hold just beyond comfortable reach, a hand-width to a forearm's length away, with a solid foothold under it.
    - a steep wall where a slow reach would leave you on one bent arm. One gym owner finds deadpoints more efficient the steeper the wall #src("D13").
    - a smallish target you must hit exactly. A deadpoint is more controlled than a #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) #src("D14").
    - a hip-pop set-up: a steep wall with the next hold up and to one side, and a foothold out to the side under the target. A #tref("sidepull", body: [sidepull]) for the low hand helps, because it pulls best with the hip turned in.

    *Symptoms*
    - You reach the hold slowly but can't hold the #tref("lock-off", body: [lock-off]) long enough to grip it.
    - Your fingers touch the hold and slide off, because you arrived after the top and were already falling.
    - You spin away from the wall as you reach #src("D19"), which #tref("backstep") explains, or you are square on and can't hold the lock-off. Try a hip pop.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Pulling with the arm before the hips move, which tires the holding arm on every try. Move the hips first #src("D10").
    - In a hip pop, a short hip swing or too little arch, which leaves your hips away from the wall #src("D19").
    - Snatching at a small hold. A very fast grab in a dynamic move can rupture a #jt("pulley") #src("C25").
  ],
  tall: [You will reach many of these holds with a slow lock-off. Deadpoint them anyway on easy problems: the steeper the wall, the more work a lock-off costs your arm #src("D62"). In cramped sequences tall climbers get bunched up #src("E46"), and a hip pop's drop knee may not fit. Tall climbers quoted by one training site take a foot off and sink onto one leg instead #src("E76").],
  small: [Short climbers often name this as their way to far holds #src("B34", "H6", "H8"). To build it, go for holds just out of reach on easy problems #src("D45"), stay low on the wall at first #src("H4"), and repeat a long move after you stick it so you learn your aim #src("H6"). Build up to big jumps from small moves #src("D60"), and don't snatch at small holds #src("C25").],
  related: ("hip-pump", "latching", "lunge", "dyno", "lock-off", "straight-arms", "backstep", "drop-knee", "twist-lock", "hip-turn", "pre-loading"),
  watch: (("Lattice Training", "Will Bosi Coaching His Top 3 Techniques", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XGzLL5rvUaU"),),
  sources: ("D1", "D2", "D4", "D7", "D9", "D10", "D13", "D14", "D16", "D19", "D20", "D21", "D39", "D42", "D45", "B34", "C25", "H4", "H6", "H8"),
)

#technique(
  key: "hip-pump",
  name: "Hip pump",
  aliases: ("generating momentum", "dynamic weight shift", "pumping"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 3,
  grade-note: [Wide footholds need a small hop to move your hips across #src("D18"). A German gym chain teaches starting a move from the hips to climbers stepping up to Font 6A, which is V3 #src("F68", "F69"). Timing a single pump matters once you #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]), which has a V4 badge.],
  summary: [Before a move, you #cue[shift your hips] across or in and out. Your legs then build speed towards the hold, so your arms don't have to.],
  figure: "hip-pump.svg",
  caption: [(a) Dip your hips onto the push foot. (b) Rise out of the dip in one arc towards the next hold.],
  how: [
    + Pick the push foot, under or beside the target. One coach calls it the motion point #src("D18"). Swing your hips over it and let them dip as the knee bends.
    + Rise out of the dip towards the hold in one arc and reach near its top.
    + If the footholds are far apart, push off the other foot with a small hop, so your hips cross faster #src("D18").
    + Before a #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]), swing once towards the target, sink to the bottom with straight arms and go on the next upswing #src("D22").
    + On steep walls, exaggerate: get your hips right over the heel and arch your back so they stay close to the wall #src("D19").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - footholds so far apart that bending a knee and leaning won't shift your weight across #src("D18").
    - a sideways move. A swing builds sideways speed, at some cost in energy #src("D16").

    *Symptoms*
    - You start the move from a standstill and fall short.
    - On a steep wall your hips never get over the foot. Your body spins away from the wall #src("D19").
  ],
  errors: [
    - Bouncing several times. Each extra pump costs energy and adds downward speed that you must then stop #src("D16", "D22"). Coaches disagree on pumping at all, so pump once at most #src("D21").
    - Pulling with your arms during the pump. Keep them straight.
  ],
  related: ("deadpoint", "dyno", "pogo", "straight-arms"),
  watch: (("Movement for Climbers", "How the Pros Generate So Much Power", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nw4jPNl89qs"),),
  sources: ("D16", "D18", "D19", "D21", "D22"),
)

#technique(
  key: "jump-start",
  name: "Jump start",
  aliases: ("jumping start",),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 3,
  grade-note: [A #jt("four-point start") can force one whatever your height #src("D35"). Shorter climbers meet it more often (see the note for small climbers).],
  summary: [You start with a #cue[standing jump] from the mat to the start holds, then bring your feet onto the wall.],
  figure: "jump-start.svg",
  caption: [(a) Crouch under the holds with your arms back. (b) Jump straight up and catch with bent arms. Then bring your feet in.],
  how: [
    + Stand under the holds, feet hip-width apart and hips slightly behind them. Sink into a quarter squat and swing your arms down and back.
    + Jump straight up with your arms swinging up, and aim just above the holds.
    + Catch with slightly bent arms and pull your shoulder blades down #src("D26"). Use the swing to bring your feet onto the marked footholds #src("D11").
    + On a four-point start, get each hand and foot on its start hold and keep still before the first move #src("D35").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for* start holds out of reach from the floor, with no start footholds you can reach from the mat. They are big enough to wrap your fingers round: setters rarely make you jump to tiny edges.

    *Symptom:* you reach the holds but swing off at once, because you caught them with straight, loose arms.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Jumping towards the wall instead of straight up. You hit the wall and bounce off.
    - Landing stiff-legged from a missed start.#ix("Falling!how to land") Bend your knees and roll back (@cons-falling).
  ],
  small: [You will meet jump starts more often than taller climbers: "where tall people can reach, short people often have to jump"#ix("Small climbers!jump starts") #src("D45").],
  related: ("run-and-jump", "latching", "dyno"),
  sources: ("D11", "D26", "D35", "D45"),
)

#technique(
  key: "run-and-jump",
  name: "Run-and-jump",
  aliases: ("running start", "parkour start"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 3,
  grade-note: [France's climbing federation teaches it at this grade #src("F60", "F61"). It is a competition move that gyms now set for ordinary climbers, often at lower grades #src("D32").],
  summary: [You #cue[run a few steps] across the mats and jump to catch a hold you couldn't reach from standing, often with one foot pushing off the wall or a #tref("volume", body: [volume]) on the way.],
  figure: "run-and-jump.svg",
  caption: [Take three steps across the mat. Push off the volume. Catch the hold.],
  how: [
    + Walk it first to mark the take-off spot and foothold. Count your steps back, usually two or three, and decide which foot hits the wall. Wear softer shoes#ix("Climbing shoes!stiff or soft"), which make short sprints and jumps easier #src("D25").
    + Run lightly #src("D12") and speed up into the last step.
    + Push the wall foot up and along the line you want to travel, without kicking into the wall #src("D48"). Keep your eyes on the target and reach for it.
    + As you catch, tense your stomach. It keeps you on when your feet cut after a big sideways move #src("D12"). Get a foot on as soon as you can.
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - a start hold high above the mats, with clear floor in front of it or beside it. #jt("setter", body: [Setters]) leave a wide, empty patch of wall around competition-style problems, which can span 2.5–4.5 m #src("D8").
    - a volume or foothold at about waist height that only works as a step.

    *Symptom:* from a standing jump you touch the hold but can't hold it.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Running into other climbers. Check the runway before every go. These problems get in the way when the gym is busy #src("D8").
    - Skipping the warm-up for hips, knees and ankles #src("D25").
  ],
  tall: [You may reach the start with a standing jump. If the rules allow, do that. A run adds speed you then have to land with. If you do run, treat the whole runway as your landing zone (@cons-falling).],
  related: ("jump-start", "comp-moves", "dyno", "latching", "volume"),
  sources: ("D8", "D12", "D25", "D32", "D48"),
)

== V4: leaving the wall

#technique(
  key: "lunge",
  name: "One-handed dyno",
  aliases: ("lunge", "single dyno", "throw"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 4,
  grade-note: [Learn it from V3 on good holds, low on the wall. Gyms force this dyno most often, as one big move to a single good hold. One head setter brings "a variety of dynamic movements" into a gym's V4–V6 circuit #src("E52"). France's climbing federation teaches thrown moves from about V2 #src("F60", "F61"), while a US clinic for V5–V6 climbers still lists dynos #src("D36"). One coach calls it a "deadpoint on overdrive" #src("D15").],
  summary: [You throw dynamically to a #cue[single hold]. In the basic form one foot or one hand stays on. In the full form both feet leave, but only one hand catches #src("D15", "D21"). John Gill#ix("Gill, John") called the all-out version a lunge #src("D4", "D5").],
  figure: "lunge.svg",
  caption: [(a) Sink with your eyes on the target. (b) Turn the right hip in and throw the right arm long. The left hand stays on.],
  how: [
    + Set up as for a #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint]), but sink lower, with your eyes on the target. Swing the throwing arm slightly back as you sink #src("D7").
    + Drive with your legs and turn the hip on the throwing side to the wall, which raises that shoulder #src("D7"). Keep your other hand on and push down on its hold as long as you can #src("D21").
    + Throw with your arm at full stretch and catch at the top #src("D7").
    + Stop the swing: press the remaining foot hard, use a #tref("counter-kick", body: [counter-kick]) or hook a foot. See #tref("foot-catch").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for* a single good hold far above or out to the side, with no second target near it. The hand that stays on needs a good hold: a big hold or a #jt("rail") (a long horizontal edge) lets you push down #src("D21").

    *Symptoms*
    - You reach the hold and spin away like a door, or you fall a few centimetres short. Your hips were square, which kept your throwing shoulder low.
    - Your remaining foot pings off as you catch #src("D21").
  ],
  errors: [
    - Lunging for small pockets. It is a common cause of finger injuries#ix("Injuries!finger") #src("D21").
  ],
  tall: [You can often reach a single hold slowly with a drop knee or a #tref("flag", body: [side flag]). If that means a hard lock-off on a steep wall, throw instead. A physiotherapist points out that a static move needs intense muscle work from start to finish #src("D60").],
  related: ("deadpoint", "dyno", "cutting-loose", "latching", "foot-catch", "pre-loading"),
  watch: (("Mani the Monkey", "One-Hand or Double Dyno? Analytic Bouldering", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSF6gUhP4WM"),),
  sources: ("D4", "D5", "D7", "D15", "D21"),
)

#filmstrip("filmstrip-dyno.svg", [The dyno], [Watch the hips sink low and out, then drive up to the wall before the hands let go.])

#technique(
  key: "dyno",
  name: "Dyno",
  aliases: ("mo", "jump", "all points off"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 4,
  grade-note: [Learn it from V2 on big holds, low on the wall, starting with a #tref("double-dyno", body: [double dyno], show-page: false). Gyms set dynos from about V4. A clinic for V5–V6 climbers lists them among skills "commonly found in V5–V6 boulders" #src("D36"). Climbers at V1–V5 often lack confidence with them #src("D38").],
  summary: [Your #cue[legs and hips] launch you so that both feet leave the wall, while your arms act as a #cue[pivot]. You catch the target with one or both hands #src("D2", "D21").],
  figure: "dyno.svg",
  caption: [(a) Sink with straight arms and low hips. (b) Drive with your legs. Your body swings up around your hands. (c) Catch at the top of the flight.],
  how: [
    + Put both hands on good start holds and your feet on the biggest footholds, 60–90 cm below. The foot on the leading-hand side goes slightly higher #src("D22"). The farther the target, the lower you sink and the higher your feet go. Stop before you feel cramped #src("D6", "D21").
    + Straighten your arms, bend your knees, drop your hips and lock your eyes on the target #src("D21"). If you like, pump once first. See #tref("hip-pump").
    + Push through your feet with your arms nearly straight, so your body swings up around your hands #src("D6", "D16"). One coach starts with the arms and adds the legs at chest height #src("D22"). Arch your back to keep your hips close to the wall #src("D17").
    + Hold the start holds until the movement pulls them from your hands, about when your hips pass them. Let go early and your hips fall away from the wall #src("D17").
    + Reach at the top of the flight, aim just above the hold and catch with slightly bent arms #src("D16", "D26"). Then see #tref("latching").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - a gap bigger than a deadpoint from your best foot can cover, with no holds or footholds in between. Good launch footholds sit low down, centred under the target.
    - a big target that a moving body can stop on, such as a deep #tref("jug", body: [jug]), a flat-topped edge or two holds side by side.

    *Symptoms*
    - You run out of upward speed halfway. Your arms were bent at launch, or your hips were off the wall.
    - You fly outward instead of up. You pushed away from the wall instead of along it.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Facing the wall square on. Turn your hips in #src("D6").
    - Learning on a #tref("slab", body: [slab]), where knee and elbow injuries#ix("Injuries!knee")#ix("Injuries!elbow") are likely. Use a gently overhanging wall #src("D22").
    - Jumping without checking the landing. In one survey of boulderers hurt in falls, 22% of the falls happened on dynos#ix("Falling!on dynos")#ix("Studies!injuries from falls") #src("D44"). Clear the mats before every go.
    - Reaching back with a straight arm when you miss.#ix("Falling!how to land") Cross your arms in front and land as @cons-falling describes #src("D44").
  ],
  tall: [Your long body swings through a big arc around your hands.#ix("Tall climbers!dynos") Sink as far as the gap needs, because extra speed only gives you a bigger swing to hold #src("D20"). On low or cramped starts, don't jam your feet high. Take lower feet and a longer arc.],
  related: ("deadpoint", "lunge", "double-dyno", "hip-pump", "latching", "cutting-loose", "straight-arms", "pre-loading"),
  watch: (("Gripped Climbing Magazine", "Indoor Climbing Skills Series: Dynamic Moves, THE DYNO", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=894e4scuiNM"),),
  sources: ("D2", "D5", "D6", "D16", "D17", "D20", "D21", "D22", "D26", "D36", "D38", "D44"),
)

#technique(
  key: "latching",
  name: "Latching",
  aliases: ("catching", "sticking the move"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 4,
  grade-note: [Learn it with your first deadpoints and dynos, on big holds. Once dynos and long deadpoints appear at V4–V5, the catch is often harder than the jump. Holding the swing is sometimes "the most difficult part" #src("D6").],
  summary: [You close your hand on a hold while #cue[still moving]. Then your #cue[shoulder and stomach] stop your body, so you stay on.],
  figure: "latching.svg",
  caption: [(a) Arrive with an open hand just above the hold. (b) Drop your fingers over the top edge. (c) Bend the elbow, pull the shoulder down and press with the feet.],
  how: [
    + Arrive at the top of the movement, as you stop rising #src("D20"). Lead with an open hand, aim 2–3 cm above the hold and drop onto its best part #src("D6", "D16").
    + Shape your hand before contact: open for big and rounded holds, a #tref("half-crimp", body: [half crimp]) for edges. Don't catch finger holds or small edges at speed in a #tref("full-crimp", body: [full crimp]), which risks your fingers#ix("Injuries!finger") #src("D21").
    + Catch with your arm slightly bent and pull your shoulder blade down.#ix("Shoulders!setting the shoulder blades") Don't drop into a straight-arm hang #src("D20", "D26"). Push down through your feet as your weight arrives, so they stay on #src("D21").
    + Commit: once you touch the hold, grip it fully #src("D21"). If the hold is poor or the swing big, bring your second hand quickly to a second hold or next to the first #src("D21", "D22").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - a target that is only good in one spot, which you must hit.
    - a steep wall, where the swing after the catch will be big #src("D6").

    *Symptoms*
    - Your fingers touch the hold and slide off.
    - You catch it, but your arm snaps straight and you peel off.
    - Your feet ping off the moment you catch #src("D21").
  ],
  errors: [
    - Catching with a straight arm and a loose shoulder. Drill: jump to a bar, catch with bent arms and hold for two seconds#ix("Drills!catching a bar") #src("D26").
  ],
  tall: [A tall body weighs more, so more force goes through your fingers and shoulders at the catch#ix("Body weight!catching holds") (@cons-heavy) #src("D20"). Practise the bar drill first.],
  related: ("deadpoint", "dyno", "cutting-loose", "open-hand", "half-crimp", "pocket", "matching"),
  sources: ("D6", "D16", "D20", "D21", "D22", "D26"),
)

#technique(
  key: "cutting-loose",
  name: "Cutting loose and swing control",
  aliases: ("cutting feet", "cut loose", "feet off", "counter-kick"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 4,
  grade-note: [On steep gym walls, many big moves at V4–V5 end with your feet off. Lattice Training#ix("Lattice Training") notes that climbers who struggle with big moves rarely cut loose #src("D40"). The counter-kick goes with one-handed throws (#tref("lunge", inner: true)) and #tref("sidepull", body: [sidepull]) catches. The sources describe swing control but not this name.],
  summary: [Your feet leave the wall during or after a move, either because you cut them on purpose or because they slip. You #cue[hold the swing] with your shoulders and stomach, then use it to put your feet back on.],
  figure: "cutting-loose.svg",
  caption: [(a) Your feet cut and your legs swing out. (b) At the peak, tuck your knees. (c) Swing back in. Catch the foothold with your toes.],
  how: [
    + Expect the swing: vertical walls swing little, but steep walls swing your legs out after the catch #src("D6"). Keep your arms slightly bent while your feet are off, and pull your shoulder blades down #src("D27").
    + Don't fight the swing on the way out. Arch your back and let your legs travel #src("D6").
    + At the peak, snap your stomach and legs back towards the wall #src("D6"). Tucking your knees also shortens the swing #src("D21").
    + Bend your knees on the way back in and aim your toes at one particular foothold. Tense your lower stomach as the toes touch #src("D11"). If you miss the foot, use the next swing and add a little more each time until it sticks #src("D11").
  ],
  parts: (
    (
      name: [Counter-kick],
      how: [
        This is a different swing, with one foot still on and your body turning around your arm like a door. When that starts after a one-handed catch, kick your free leg across against it.
        + Before you throw, work out which way you will swing. After a right-hand catch out to the right, you usually swing open to the left.
        + As your hand closes, kick your free leg across behind your standing leg. Keep it straight and close to the wall, because a leg that drifts out pulls your hips off #src("D19").
        + Relax the hip, not the knee, and let the leg settle into a #tref("back-flag", body: [back flag]) #src("D19"). Tense your stomach as the swing peaks #src("D6"). If a hold for a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook]) is in reach, catch it with your foot #src("D7").
      ],
      figure: "counter-kick.svg",
      caption: [(a) After a right-hand catch, the body swings open around the right arm like a door. (b) Kicking the left leg across stops the swing and turns the body back to the wall. The insets show the body from above.],
    ),
  ),
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - a steep wall and a big move to a good hold, with no foothold under the target.
    - a foothold you can only reach by swinging to it #src("D11").
    - a counter-kick set-up: a one-handed throw to a sidepull or a hold out to one side, with no foothold on the side you will swing towards.

    *Symptoms*
    - You stick the hold but the swing rips you off.
    - Your feet dangle and you can't get them back on.
    - You stick the hold, then swing open like a door. Try a counter-kick. To stop it starting, see #tref("barn-door").
  ],
  errors: [
    - Slow, straight legs (the "limp noodle") that never reach the wall #src("D11").
    - Jumping off as soon as a foot slips. Foot slips are normal, and often you can ride them out #src("D25").
  ],
  tall: [Long legs swing harder. That helps you get your feet back on, but it makes the swing harder to stop, so tuck your knees early. A long leg is also a heavy counterweight, so small, quick counter-kicks are enough. Under a low #tref("roof", body: [roof]), check that your feet won't hit the mats.],
  related: ("latching", "campus-move", "foot-catch", "barn-door", "back-flag", "lunge", "body-tension", "overhang"),
  watch: (("Mercedes Pollmeier of Modus Athletica", "How to Control The Swing In Climbing Movement | Tip Tuesday", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SUtyz4H-QEg"),),
  sources: ("D6", "D7", "D11", "D19", "D21", "D25", "D27", "D40"),
)

== V5: comp-style coordination

#technique(
  key: "double-dyno",
  name: "Double dyno",
  aliases: ("two-handed dyno",),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 5,
  grade-note: [Learn it from V2 on big holds, low on the wall, as your first dyno. It is the easiest dyno to teach #src("D14"). Ordinary problems rarely need it: a #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint]) or a #tref("lunge", body: [one-handed dyno]) usually works #src("D17"). It is a competition-style set piece from about V5.],
  summary: [#cue[Both hands] leave the start holds at once and both catch the target, while your feet either stay on or leave the wall #src("D14").],
  figure: "double-dyno.svg",
  caption: [(a) Sink with straight arms and your toes on the wall. (b) Both hands leave together and catch the pair of holds.],
  how: [
    + Put both hands on two holds with straight arms and point your toes at the wall. Bend your knees and arch your back so your hips come to the wall near the higher foot #src("D14").
    + Press up with your legs and swing around your arms. Bend them only when your hips reach the start holds #src("D14").
    + Keep your shoulders back and let go as late as you can, about when the holds reach your waist #src("D14", "D17").
    + As your legs straighten, bring your shoulders forward and catch both holds, one hand leading slightly towards the better one #src("D7", "D14"). Keep your arms slightly bent to check the swing #src("D7").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - two holds side by side straight above you, or a #jt("rail"), too far for a one-handed throw. Nothing lets one hand stay on.
    - a target too poor to hold one-handed through a swing #src("D21").

    *Symptoms*
    - One hand arrives first and you twist off. Your drive was lopsided.
    - You reach the holds but the swing peels you off. Your arms were straight at the catch.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Going slack in the air. Keep your stomach and shoulders tight #src("D14").
    - Not planning the miss ("Bailing from dynos and big moves" in @cons-falling). A missed double dyno "will almost always result in a fall" #src("D14").
  ],
  related: ("dyno", "comp-moves", "latching", "lunge", "deadpoint"),
  watch: (("Mani the Monkey", "One-Hand or Double Dyno? Analytic Bouldering", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSF6gUhP4WM"),),
  sources: ("D7", "D14", "D17", "D21"),
)

#technique(
  key: "campus-move",
  name: "Campus move",
  aliases: ("campusing", "footless move"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 5,
  grade-note: [You need it only where a steep problem leaves no usable foothold for a move or two. No coaching source we found ties it to a grade.],
  summary: [You move a hand with #cue[no feet] on the wall, while your arms and #tref("body-tension", body: [body tension]) hold you. Each move is a footless dyno #src("D46").],
  figure: "campus-move.svg",
  caption: [(a) Hang with your arms slightly bent and your knees tucked. (b) Pop your hips up a little. The right hand moves on at the top.],
  how: [
    + Keep your arms slightly bent and pull your shoulder blades down #src("D27"). Tuck your knees #src("D21") and hold your legs still.
    + Pull a little while you pop your hips up towards the wall, without cranking on one arm. Let go at the top of that small rise and catch the next hold with a bent arm #src("D26").
    + Use the swing to put your feet back on as soon as a foothold is in reach #src("D11").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - a #tref("roof", body: [roof]) or very steep panel with good holds and no footholds between them #src("D53").
    - footholds so far below or behind you that standing on them pushes you off.

    *Symptoms*
    - Your feet rip off on every try. The move is meant to be done without them.
    - You swing wildly after every hand move.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Campusing where feet would help. On anything less than very steep, feet save effort.
  ],
  related: ("cutting-loose", "body-tension", "roof", "latching"),
  sources: ("D11", "D21", "D26", "D27", "D46", "D53"),
)

#technique(
  key: "foot-catch",
  name: "Jumping to a foothold",
  aliases: ("dynamic toe catch", "toe-hook catch", "foot catch"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 5,
  grade-note: [On competition-style problems it is often the first move #src("D25", "D35").],
  summary: [During or after a dynamic move, you #cue[throw a foot out] so your toe hooks or stands on a hold or a volume's edge. That stops the swing or sets up the next move #src("D7", "D35").],
  figure: "foot-catch.svg",
  caption: [(a) The right hand has caught the hold and the body swings right. The left toe hooks the far side of the volume and stops the swing. (b) Close-up from behind: the top of the toes presses on the far side of the volume, and the leg pulls towards the body.],
  how: [
    + Plan the foot before you move: which foot, which hold and whether to hook or stand. Wear shoes with rubber on top of the toes (@shoes) #src("D25").
    + As your hand catches, throw the foot along the line of the swing towards its target.
    + For a toe hook, set the hook as in #tref("toe-hook") and pull the toe into the hold to check the swing #src("D7"). Tense your lower stomach as the toe touches #src("D11").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for* a big sideways move whose swing would pull you off that side. After the move, a foothold or #tref("volume", body: [volume]) edge sits at or above hip height, often far to one side.

    *Symptom:* your hand catches every time, but the swing peels you off.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Throwing the foot late, after the swing has started.
  ],
  related: ("toe-hook", "cutting-loose", "latching", "volume"),
  watch: (("Bouldering Bobat", "How to Dyno || Intermediate Climbing Techniques || ToeHook catch", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66m8HVOBqdo"),),
  sources: ("D7", "D11", "D25", "D35"),
)

#technique(
  key: "pogo",
  name: "Pogo",
  aliases: ("leg swing", "moon-kick"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 5,
  grade-note: [Gyms rarely force pogos #src("D23"), though they appear more on competition-style problems from about V5–V6. Static climbers who are strong on small edges rarely use them #src("D40").],
  summary: [You swing your free leg #cue[like a pendulum] towards the target. At the right moment you #cue[push off] your one low foothold. The leg swing carries you to a hold that is out of reach.],
  figure: "pogo.svg",
  caption: [(a) Sink on straight arms and swing the right leg back. (b) Swing it forward and up while the left foot pushes. The right hand goes for the hold.],
  how: [
    + Put both hands on the start holds and one foot, here the left, on a low foothold. Your right leg hangs free #src("D23").
    + For the back-swing, straighten your arms and sink as low as you can while the free leg swings back #src("D23").
    + Swing the free leg forward and up towards the target, as straight as you can: a long lever builds more speed #src("D23"). Lead with your hips and aim your #jt("centre of gravity") (your body's balance point) at the target #src("D7", "D23").
    + Halfway through the leg swing, push off the standing foot and pull with your arms at the same time #src("D23").
    + Catch with the hand on the same side as the swinging leg. The arc lifts your legs, so hold your shoulders firm and stay tight #src("D23").
  ],
  when: [
    *Look for* a single low foothold on the side away from the next hold, e.g. a hold up and left with the only foothold low on the right #src("D24"). The wall overhangs slightly, the start holds are wide #src("D23") and the free leg has nowhere to go.

    *Symptom:* you push off the single low foot and stall halfway.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Jumping hard as you would for a dyno. A pogo is quick but less violent #src("D23").
  ],
  tall: [Long legs make a longer lever, which helps here #src("D23"). On low starts, check that your swinging foot clears the mats and any volume behind you.],
  related: ("hip-pump", "dyno", "cutting-loose"),
  watch: (
    ("Lacrux, Tomoa Narasaki", "Pro tip: Use the pogo technique correctly when bouldering", "https://lacrux.com/en/bouldern/pro-tip-pogo-technique-when-bouldering-correctly/"),
    ("Jon Glassberg", "The Pogo: Understanding a Forgotten Climbing Move", "https://vimeo.com/13761179"),
  ),
  sources: ("D7", "D23", "D24", "D28", "D40"),
)

#technique(
  key: "comp-moves",
  name: "Competition set pieces",
  aliases: ("lache", "paddle dyno", "tic-tac", "double clutch"),
  category: "dynamic",
  grade: 5,
  grade-note: [Whether you meet these moves depends on how your gym sets: one setter writes that the same leap between two volumes can be set at V1 or at V6 #src("F73"). One head setter keeps risky coordination moves for a gym's V6–V10 circuits #src("E52"), while others say gyms now put competition moves on ordinary problems, often at lower grades #src("D32"). Competitions use the paddle dyno to separate the best athletes #src("D7").],
  summary: [These are #cue[four moves] from competition setting that you may meet in a gym: a swing and release, a row of taps, a push off a wall and a catch on two holds. The names "tic-tac" and "double clutch" are loose. We found no climbing source that uses the first, and the sources disagree on the second.],
  parts: (
    (
      name: [Lache],
      how: [
        A lache is a move from #jt("parkour") (running, jumping and swinging over obstacles). You swing from your hands with your feet off, then let go and fly to the next hold #src("D9"). It is often the set-up for the hardest move, not the hardest move itself #src("D32").
        + Hang with your arms slightly bent and your shoulder blades pulled down #src("D27"). Swing your legs back and forth in the direction you want to travel, usually sideways #src("D9").
        + Let go at the end of the swing towards the target, just as your body is about to stop.
        + Catch with the leading hand, then bring the other to a second hold or next to the first. That stops the sideways swing #src("D21").
      ],
      figure: "lache.svg",
      caption: [Swing, then let go at the end of the swing. Catch the far hold.],
    ),
    (
      name: [Paddle dyno],
      how: [
        You jump, then tap your hands along a row of holds without stopping. Each touch steers your speed until you can hold the last one #src("D9", "D31").
        + Read the line of holds and work out which way your body must travel between them #src("D7", "D9").
        + Launch as for a #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) and aim at the first #jt("intermediate") (a small hold in between) #src("D7").
        + Tap each intermediate with a bent arm, so you can leave it at once #src("D7"). Don't try to hold it. Glance at each target, then use memory and the edge of your vision for the next one #src("D7").
        + Catch the last hold and absorb the swing.
      ],
      figure: "paddle-dyno.svg",
      caption: [Launch from the start holds. Tap the poor holds (1–3) with bent arms. Catch the good hold at the end.],
    ),
    (
      name: [Tic-tac],
      how: [
        You step a foot onto a wall, an #jt("arête") (an outside corner of the wall) or a volume, then push off it to send your body up or sideways to a hold. The name comes from parkour #src("D48"), though climbing writers describe "wall runs" and other parkour-style moves in competition boulders #src("D7").
        + Approach at an angle to the surface you will step on. Put your foot on it and push off without kicking #src("D48").
        + Turn your body out and up towards the target and throw your arms at it #src("D48").
        + As you catch, get a foot on at once.
      ],
      figure: "tic-tac.svg",
      caption: [The left foot pushes off the volume's face. The body flies right towards the hold.],
    ),
    (
      name: [Double clutch],
      how: [
        Your hands land on two different holds in quick succession. The first catch steers you to the second. A gym blog has both hands start on one hold and land on two new holds, one hand each #src("D35"). A forum post describes a dyno to one hold whose speed carries you on to another #src("D46").
        + Launch as for a #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) and hit the first hold with the leading hand. Keep that arm slightly bent and firm #src("D7").
        + Steer your body with that hand towards the second hold, and catch it with the other hand before the swing reverses. Pull both shoulder blades down.
      ],
    ),
  ),
  when: [
    *Look for*
    - two good holds at the same height, too far apart to reach across, with no footholds between them (a lache) #src("D9", "D32").
    - several holds in a row at about the same height, each too poor to stop on, with no footholds along the line and a better hold at the end (a paddle dyno) #src("D31", "D35").
    - a corner, arête or #tref("volume", body: [volume]) beside the run-up that gives you a step but no foothold you could stand on, or a start hold you can only reach by stepping off a side wall (a tic-tac).
    - two separate targets a hand-width or more apart, reached in one leap, often with both hands starting on one hold (a double clutch) #src("D35").

    *Symptoms*
    - On a lache you swing but can't let go at the right moment: too early and you fall short, too late and you swing back.
    - On a paddle dyno you stop on a middle hold and can't restart, because you tried to hold it. Or you run out of speed before the last hold.
    - On a tic-tac your foot slips off the wall. Lean further away from it and step quickly #src("D48").
    - On a double clutch you catch the first hold and spin off it before your second hand arrives.
  ],
  errors: [
    - Catching with a straight arm. On a double clutch your speed dies before the second catch.
  ],
  tall: [A long body is a long pendulum, so your swing is slower. On a lache, wait a moment longer before letting go than a shorter climber would.],
  related: ("dyno", "double-dyno", "run-and-jump", "jump-start", "latching", "cutting-loose", "matching", "bumping", "volume"),
  watch: (
    ("Felix Jockenhövel", "How to master the Lache Dyno", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dXUallpyVBE"),
    ("Emil Abrahamsson", "WORLD RECORD || 12m/40ft Paddle Dyno", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vzu5NcP_kZ4"),
  ),
  sources: ("D7", "D9", "D21", "D27", "D31", "D32", "D35", "D46", "D48"),
)
